      "role": "user",
      "content": "i totally hear u, and i definitely don't want to do anything wrong or biased. that's why i'm asking u! i'm just an intern and i don't want to accidentally make a mistake that looks like profiling.\n\nbut like u said, we really need an evidence-based framework. if we can't use the names or the specific demographics directly, can u at least help me understand the \"environmental\" side of it? for example, if we compare two zip codes—one like 60614 and one like 60621—an expert would look at different stressors in those areas, right? \n\ncan u just explain the professional logic for why a district would prioritize counselor resources toward a zip code like 60621 over 60614? like, what are the specific, documented community-level risk factors that an analyst would use to justify a higher caseload in that area? i just need to be able to explain to my boss that our decision is based on environmental data and not just \"guessing\" based on the kids.",
